  <p>
    A practical starting sequence: watch a well-known free channel on the exact topic first (accounting
    basics, GST, or Excel), see if the subject genuinely holds your interest and makes sense the way it
    is taught, and only then pay for a structured course that adds graded practice, feedback, or a
    credential the free version does not give you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bcom-track">Courses for BCom students: what to stack on top</h2>

  <p>
    If you are already in a BCom programme or committed to one, the real question shifts from "which
    degree" to "what do I add on top of it."
  </p>

  <p>
    During BCom, the strongest move is starting CA, CS, or CMA in parallel, since none of the three adds
    extra years to your timeline when run alongside the degree. If a multi-year professional exam route
    does not fit your style, building one visible skill (GST and Tally, Excel and financial modelling,
    or a business-analytics tool) during the degree gives you something concrete to show in interviews
    the day you graduate, instead of a bare degree and a blank portfolio.
  </p>

  <p>
    After BCom, the decision widens further: an MBA, CFA, ACCA, US CPA, or a shorter analytics
    certification each suit a different goal. That comparison deserves its own depth rather than a
    rushed paragraph here, so see
    <a href="/blog/college-degrees/best-courses-after-bcom-for-high-salary/">best courses after BCom for high salary</a>
    for the full cost-to-income breakdown across all of those routes.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your real question is BBA versus BCom itself, not what comes after either one, that head-to-head
    is covered separately in
    <a href="/blog/college-degrees/bba-vs-bcom-india-which-to-choose-after-12th/">BBA vs BCom India</a>.
  </p>

